WEBVTT

00:00:00.268 --> 00:00:02.948
I wanted an email app that didn't suck,

00:00:02.948 --> 00:00:04.108
because of ads,

00:00:04.108 --> 00:00:07.578
UX from the 90s or good old turtle speed.

00:00:07.724 --> 00:00:10.154
So I started building Rukoo.

00:00:10.665 --> 00:00:12.865
It brings your accounts into one inbox,

00:00:12.865 --> 00:00:15.215
with your calendars in the same window.

00:00:17.702 --> 00:00:19.075
See everything together,

00:00:19.075 --> 00:00:20.972
or one account on its own.

00:00:22.362 --> 00:00:24.282
Then I started thinking about the emails

00:00:24.282 --> 00:00:26.392
I didn't want to handle myself.

00:00:26.658 --> 00:00:28.018
I already had agents running,

00:00:28.018 --> 00:00:29.431
with their own tools.

00:00:29.431 --> 00:00:32.288
So the agent panel ended up next to the inbox.

00:00:33.076 --> 00:00:36.436
Connect Claude Code, Codex, Hermes or OpenClaw,

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:38.034
or build your own connection.

00:00:38.034 --> 00:00:40.381
Open an email, ask your agent to work on it,

00:00:40.381 --> 00:00:41.704
and keep going.

00:00:46.786 --> 00:00:48.186
The conversation keeps running

00:00:48.186 --> 00:00:50.096
while you deal with the rest.

00:00:51.978 --> 00:00:53.498
What Rukoo adds is access

00:00:53.498 --> 00:00:55.422
to what I'm working on right now.

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:56.755
Through its MCP server,

00:00:56.755 --> 00:00:58.915
an agent can read the email I have open

00:00:58.915 --> 00:01:00.302
and the draft I'm writing,

00:01:00.302 --> 00:01:02.905
and put a reply right into the composer.

00:01:07.722 --> 00:01:10.912
If I change that draft, it reads my changes too.

00:01:15.697 --> 00:01:18.604
Rukoo also categorizes incoming mail.

00:01:18.604 --> 00:01:20.337
Describe what belongs in each category,

00:01:20.337 --> 00:01:22.657
and Jev, TypeSafe's decision model,

00:01:22.657 --> 00:01:24.954
sorts what the rules aren't sure about.

00:01:25.093 --> 00:01:27.173
If a message lands in the wrong category,

00:01:27.173 --> 00:01:30.083
correct it, also for that sender's future mail.

00:01:33.053 --> 00:01:35.266
The latest addition is meeting booking.

00:01:35.266 --> 00:01:36.626
Choose which calendars count,

00:01:36.626 --> 00:01:39.336
and the hours and meeting types people can book.

00:01:40.256 --> 00:01:41.323
Someone picks a time,

00:01:41.323 --> 00:01:43.993
and Rukoo adds the meeting to your calendar.

00:01:46.248 --> 00:01:47.902
And the person you send that link to

00:01:47.902 --> 00:01:49.612
can hand it to their agent.

00:01:50.041 --> 00:01:52.068
The page has instructions for assistants,

00:01:52.068 --> 00:01:54.858
an API and an MCP endpoint.

00:01:55.833 --> 00:01:59.543
An agent can find an available time and book it.

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:09.402
Rukoo runs on Windows, Linux, macOS and Android.

00:02:09.402 --> 00:02:10.229
On my phone,

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:12.686
I chat with the agents running on my desktop.

00:02:18.263 --> 00:02:20.183
It's free and open source.

00:02:20.183 --> 00:02:21.010
Download it from GitHub,

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.160
and try the demo account first.

00:02:23.921 --> 00:02:25.841
There's no iOS app yet.

00:02:25.841 --> 00:02:28.161
If anyone wants to prompt one into existence,

00:02:28.161 --> 00:02:29.311
please feel free.
